  "answer": "**Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the peak.**\n\n- **Latest (August 2026):** Sydney rents were up **3.5% year on year**. The national figure was **3.6%**.\n- **Peak:** Sydney's annual rent growth hit **8.8% in March 2024**, so it has roughly halved since then.\n- **Recent trend:** Growth has been flat at about 3.3–3.5% since late 2025. It was 3.2% in August 2025 and has barely moved since.\n- **Monthly pace:** Sydney rents have risen about 0.2–0.4% each month through 2026, with the latest reading at 0.3% in August.\n\nSo the situation is far less acute than in 2023–24, when rents were rising at close to 9% a year. But rents are still climbing, and tenants are not getting relief in absolute terms. Whether 3.5% counts as \"bad\" depends on wages and incomes, which I haven't checked here. If you want that comparison, I can pull wage growth from the ABS Wage Price Index and set it against rents.",
